macOS: %gs:0x20, %gs:0x08 and %gs:0x10 are PE-owned while PE code runs (Tib.FiberData, StackBase,
StackLimit) and the host's while unix code runs (the pthread QoS slot, the errno pointer, the MIG
reply port); the PE ntdll reads the stack bounds from the slots.

Windows keeps NT_TIB.FiberData, StackBase and StackLimit at %gs:0x20, 0x08 and 0x10, and a program that
runs its own fiber system (Boost.Context style, Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024) writes all three on
every switch and reads them back. On this tree %gs is the host thread block for PE code too, where those
slots are the pthread QoS class, the errno pointer and the MIG reply port, and patch 0009 handed PE code
Wine's Tib.FiberData after every syscall (the sim died in its "FiberError" assertion at BOOT_INIT, M4,
macOS 27.0, engine r18, 2026-10-08), while the sim's stack base written into %gs:0x08 was read as the
errno pointer by the first unix routine that touched errno (ntdll.so, movl (%rax) after __error(),
rax = 0x15010000, 2026-10-09, gin/private/notes/msfs-2024-2026-10-08.md sections C, G and H).

Now the three slots take turns like %gs:0x68 (0011): what PE code last left there goes into the TEB's own
Tib.FiberData, StackBase and StackLimit on entry to unix code and the host's values come back (host_qos at
0x340, host_errno at 0x350, host_mig at 0x358 in amd64_thread_data); the reverse on every return to PE
code. The TEB fields are the natural store: Wine's unix side and kernelbase's fibers already read and
write them, and ntoskrnl keeps its KTHREAD in teb->Instrumentation[1], which the first form of this
patch overwrote (winedevice.exe read [StackBase+0x18] and died at every boot, r18ps and r18s2). Two rules make this hold at every instruction: the entry swap runs on the kernel stack and
the return swap on the user stack, so "%rsp inside the syscall region" and "the slots are the host's"
always agree, and every swap is compare-based, each slot moved only while it still holds the other
side's value, so a swap can run twice without stashing the wrong value. Signal handlers follow the same
rules: init_handler puts the host's values in (the handler is unix code: wait_suspend reads errno
first thing), leave_handler puts PE's back when the context it returns to is outside the signal stack
and the syscall region, which also covers a handler that redirected there (an exception to dispatch, a
syscall fault returning to its caller). user_mode_callback_return swaps nothing: NtCallbackReturn is a
syscall and the dispatcher already did it (the first trial saved the host's values as PE's there, and
a second trial's signal handlers read errno through PE's stack base: the 32-bit Steam died in
wait_suspend, "nested exception on signal stack", r18ps, 2026-10-09).

call_init_thunk keeps the host's values, the unix guard-page move writes Tib.StackLimit as before,
kernelbase's SwitchToFiber writes the slots as it writes the fields, and the PE ntdll's exception dispatcher (is_valid_frame, the unwind end checks) reads the bounds
from the slots, which is where Windows reads them, so a fiber stack a program announced there is
accepted. D3DMetal's host code called directly from PE code sees PE's values in those slots, as it did
before this patch whenever such a program ran. Trial, branch msfs-gsbase. Third form (the first two trials
0022 fiber slot and 0023 stack bounds, then the Instrumentation-based form of this patch).
